What the conversion changed. The weights are the original's, in float32. The forward pass was written again in
PyTorch for one fixed chunk of 16 kHz audio (2 s in both files) and exported with litert-torch
0.9.4 (torch 2.11.0). It computes the original's function in float32, rearranged for a GPU:
- the STFT, the inverse STFT and the joining of the chunks run outside the graphs, on the host;
- the graphs return the phase as the two arguments of the original's final atan2;
- the Mamba layers run on 4D tensors without flips: the backward direction is a reverse scan with an anti-causal
  convolution; the time scan steps through the frames one at a time in reuse_16k_2s_fp32.tflite and in blocks of
  8 frames in reuse_16k_2s_fastload_fp32.tflite; the frequency scan works in blocks of 8 positions;
- InstanceNorm and LayerNorm compute in a scaled-down range, and the Mamba output path is scaled before its gate, so
  that float16 arithmetic on a GPU stays in range; softplus, PReLU and the sub-pixel transposed convolution are
  written in equivalent forms that the LiteRT GPU accepts;
- 2 dilated convolutions of the encoder run in 2 tiles each along time, so that no im2col
  buffer reaches the 1 GiB that LiteRT's convolution allows for dilated convolutions on Android.

The scans follow the reference scan of mamba_ssm, selective_scan_ref (state-spaces/mamba, commit
a14b1dff0454a3bc27d9eb31355dc01e4b2490ec, Apache License 2.0, Copyright 2023 Tri Dao, Albert Gu).
